{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"alias":"omphacite-desertlike-coney","creation_channel":"cli","description":"# Reduce the macOS pytest cell to a platform-compatibility subset\n\n## Context / Problem\nThe Gerrit Verified Gate's critical path is the macOS pytest cell. It runs the FULL default\nsuite plus the integration tier, while every other gate job finishes by ~20 min. Measured over\nfour recent successful runs the macOS py3.13 cell is ~2140-2486s and is the sole long pole\n(gate ~43 min). On the IDENTICAL test selection the macOS runner is ~3.7x slower per test than\nUbuntu (macOS ~2140s vs Ubuntu ~572-595s), attributable to per-test process-spawn + git-store\nfixture overhead — the CPU-bound AST/policy scanners are equal/faster on macOS. Running full\nbreadth on the slow runner buys latency, not confidence.\n\n## Approach\nKeep the full suite on Linux (Ubuntu 3.11/3.12/3.13 unchanged) and reduce the macOS cell to the\ntests whose behaviour genuinely differs between Darwin and Linux: file locking (flock/fcntl/\nmkdir), atomic directory/rename, subprocess timeout (gtimeout vs timeout), and cross-process git\ncontention. macOS keeps its existing `integration` tier step (cross-process concurrency\nend-to-end) and runs a new `platform_compat`-marked subset in place of the full default suite.\nSelection is centralised in ONE auditable place — a path-keyed auto-marker in tests/conftest.py\n— rather than per-file markers, so the macOS suite cannot silently drift. This fits the goal of\ncutting CI runtime without cutting confidence: Linux still runs everything.\n\n## Scope\n- pyproject.toml — register a `platform_compat` marker in the `[tool.pytest.ini_options]`\n  `markers` list (the block currently ends at the `e2e:` entry).\n- tests/conftest.py — extend the existing `pytest_collection_modifyitems` hook (which already\n  auto-applies the `external` marker by path) to also apply `platform_compat` by path, driven by\n  a documented `_PLATFORM_COMPAT_PATH_FRAGMENTS` tuple (store/lock/atomic/timeout/snapshot/\n  contention seams).\n- .github/workflows/_build-and-test.yml — change the `DEFAULT_SUITE_MARKS` env expression\n  (currently the two-branch ternary at the \"Run the default suite\" step) so the macOS cell\n  selects `platform_compat and not external`; Ubuntu keeps its existing selection. The macOS\n  integration-tier step is unchanged.\n- tests/unit/test_ci_workflow_parity.py — update\n  `test_repo_policy_nodes_run_only_in_the_existing_primary_cell` (its regex hardcodes the old\n  two-branch `DEFAULT_SUITE_MARKS`) for the new three-branch expression, and add a guard that\n  the macOS subset still collects the core OS-sensitive files.\n\n## Testing\n- Collect and run `-m \"platform_compat and not external\"` locally; confirm it selects the\n  store-lock / timeout / snapshot / contention tests and passes.\n- Run tests/unit/test_ci_workflow_parity.py (the CI-shape guard) to green.\n- A new guard test asserts a representative set of platform-sensitive files (store lock,\n  stamped_lock, timeout_budget, repo_snapshot, event_append_index_lock, atomic_ticket_dir_create)\n  collect under the marker, so pattern drift fails loudly rather than silently emptying the macOS\n  suite (the failure path).\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] A `platform_compat` marker is registered in pyproject.toml and `pytest -m platform_compat`\n      collects a non-empty set (verified by collection + the new parity guard test).\n- [ ] tests/conftest.py auto-applies `platform_compat` by path via\n      `_PLATFORM_COMPAT_PATH_FRAGMENTS`, and the macOS `DEFAULT_SUITE_MARKS` branch in\n      _build-and-test.yml is `platform_compat and not external` (verified by\n      test_repo_policy_nodes_run_only_in_the_existing_primary_cell).\n- [ ] The Ubuntu default-suite selection is unchanged — the primary/non-primary branches still\n      read `not integration and not external` and `... and not repo_policy` (verified by the same\n      parity test).\n- [ ] tests/unit/test_ci_workflow_parity.py passes in full.\n- [ ] The platform_compat subset runs green locally via\n      `pytest -m \"platform_compat and not external and not integration\"`.","id":"3ba8-49d5-fee5-445d","parent_id":"","priority":2,"tags":[],"ticket_type":"story","title":"Reduce the macOS pytest cell to a platform-compatibility subset"},"env_id":"b8a354e0-12c3-4cf2-bbf1-fcc9685d5126","event_type":"CREATE","timestamp":1787705222556769001,"uuid":"8277ce10-1286-49cf-b623-dba6e03cfe63"}